<body>

<header>
  <div style="max-width:1200px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 32px;">
    <h1>Active Satellites in Earth Orbit</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">2026 Catalog Analysis — 14,875 Tracked Active Spacecraft</div>
    <div class="meta">Source: CelesTrak / NORAD TLE Catalog &nbsp;|&nbsp; Epoch range: 26 Feb – 30 Mar 2026 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Report generated April 2026</div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- KPI Strip -->
<div class="kpi-strip">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="value">14,875</div>
    <div class="label">Total Active Satellites</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="value">94.6%</div>
    <div class="label">LEO Share</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="value">10,083</div>
    <div class="label">Starlink Constellation</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="value">53.16°</div>
    <div class="label">Median Inclination</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="value">13,461</div>
    <div class="label">Launched Since 2020</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="container">

<!-- SECTION 1: Catalog Overview -->
<div class="section" id="overview">
  <h2>1. Catalog Overview &amp; Key Figures</h2>
  <p>The CelesTrak active-satellite catalog contains <strong>14,875 operational spacecraft</strong> with orbital element sets generated between 26 February and 30 March 2026. The catalog spans launch years from 1964 through early 2026, though the fleet is overwhelmingly modern: <strong>90.5% of all active satellites were launched in 2020 or later</strong> (13,461 of 14,875).</p>

  <h3>High-Level Statistical Profile</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Parameter</th><th class="num">Mean</th><th class="num">Median</th><th class="num">Std Dev</th><th class="num">Min</th><th class="num">Max</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Mean Motion (rev/day)</td><td class="num">14.33</td><td class="num">15.27</td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">0.24</td><td class="num">16.40</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inclination (°)</td><td class="num">60.04</td><td class="num">53.16</td><td class="num">23.88</td><td class="num">0.00</td><td class="num">149.64</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Eccentricity</td><td class="num">0.00229</td><td class="num">0.00016</td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">0.0</td><td class="num">0.896</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout blue">
    <strong>Key insight:</strong> The median mean motion of 15.27 rev/day (corresponding to ~550 km altitude) reflects Starlink's dominant shell. The median eccentricity of 0.00016 confirms that the vast majority of active satellites occupy near-circular orbits. 99.0% of all satellites have eccentricity &lt; 0.01.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 2: Orbital Regime Classification -->
<div class="section" id="orbit-regime">
  <h2>2. Orbital Regime Classification</h2>

  <div class="method-note">
    <strong>Classification method:</strong> Satellites are classified using MEAN_MOTION thresholds: <strong>LEO</strong> = mean motion &gt; 11 rev/day (~2,000 km altitude and below); <strong>GEO</strong> = 0.85–1.5 rev/day (~35,786 km); <strong>MEO</strong> = 1.5–11 rev/day; <strong>HEO</strong> = eccentricity &gt; 0.25 and mean motion &lt; 11 (highly elliptical orbits such as Molniya).
  </div>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-box">
      <h4>Orbital Regime Distribution</h4>
      <canvas id="regimeDonut" height="280"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <h4>Regime Breakdown (Count &amp; Share)</h4>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Regime</th><th class="num">Count</th><th class="num">Share</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="highlight-row"><td>LEO</td><td class="num">14,073</td><td class="num">94.6%</td><td>Dominated by Starlink (71.6% of LEO)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GEO</td><td class="num">595</td><td class="num">4.0%</td><td>Comms, weather, military</td></tr>
          <tr><td>MEO</td><td class="num">171</td><td class="num">1.1%</td><td>Navigation constellations (GPS, BeiDou, Galileo, GLONASS)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>HEO</td><td class="num">36</td><td class="num">0.2%</td><td>Molniya-type &amp; science missions</td></tr>
        </tbody>
        <tfoot>
          <tr style="font-weight:700;border-top:2px solid var(--border);"><td>Total</td><td class="num">14,875</td><td class="num">100%</td><td></td></tr>
        </tfoot>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>Low Earth Orbit hosts <strong>94.6% of all active satellites</strong>. Within LEO, Starlink alone accounts for <strong>10,083 spacecraft — 71.6% of the LEO population</strong> and 67.8% of all active satellites globally. The GEO belt holds 595 satellites (4.0%), while MEO — home to navigation constellations — contains just 171 (1.1%). Only 36 satellites operate in highly elliptical orbits.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3: Constellation Analysis -->
<div class="section" id="constellation-analysis">
  <h2>3. Major Constellation &amp; Operator Breakdown</h2>
  <p>Constellation membership is identified by parsing the <code>OBJECT_NAME</code> prefix. Satellites not matching any known constellation prefix are grouped as "Other."</p>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-box full">
      <h4>Top 12 Constellations by Satellite Count</h4>
      <canvas id="constellationBar" height="110"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>#</th><th>Constellation</th><th class="num">Satellites</th><th class="num">Share of Total</th><th>Orbit</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr class="highlight-row"><td>1</td><td>Starlink (SpaceX)</td><td class="num">10,083</td><td class="num">67.8%</td><td>LEO ~550 km</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>OneWeb</td><td class="num">651</td><td class="num">4.4%</td><td>LEO ~1,200 km</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>Yaogan (PRC military/civil)</td><td class="num">165</td><td class="num">1.1%</td><td>LEO/SSO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Cosmos (Russia)</td><td class="num">126</td><td class="num">0.8%</td><td>Various</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>Flock (Planet Labs)</td><td class="num">119</td><td class="num">0.8%</td><td>LEO/SSO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6</td><td>Iridium</td><td class="num">80</td><td class="num">0.5%</td><td>LEO ~780 km</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7</td><td>Lemur (Spire)</td><td class="num">76</td><td class="num">0.5%</td><td>LEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>BeiDou (PRC nav)</td><td class="num">55</td><td class="num">0.4%</td><td>MEO/GEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9</td><td>ICEYE (SAR)</td><td class="num">45</td><td class="num">0.3%</td><td>LEO ~570 km</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10</td><td>GPS / NAVSTAR (US)</td><td class="num">39</td><td class="num">0.3%</td><td>MEO ~20,200 km</td></tr>
      <tr><td>11</td><td>Hawk (HawkEye 360)</td><td class="num">36</td><td class="num">0.2%</td><td>LEO</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12</td><td>Intelsat</td><td class="num">28</td><td class="num">0.2%</td><td>GEO</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Starlink dominance:</strong> A single operator — SpaceX — accounts for more than two-thirds of all active satellites. This concentration fundamentally skews every aggregate statistic: the median orbital inclination (53.16°) matches Starlink's primary shell, and the median mean motion (15.27 rev/day) reflects its ~550 km altitude. Excluding Starlink, the median inclination shifts to 87.90° (polar/SSO), revealing a very different underlying distribution.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4: Launch Trends (MOST IMPORTANT) -->
<div class="section" id="launch-trends">
  <h2>4. Launch Year Trends</h2>
  <p>Launch year is extracted from the first four characters of <code>OBJECT_ID</code> (international designator). This reflects the year each currently active satellite was launched — not the total launched, since deorbited or failed satellites are excluded.</p>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-box full">
      <h4>Annual Launches of Currently Active Satellites (2010–2026)</h4>
      <canvas id="launchTrend" height="100"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-box full">
      <h4>Cumulative Active Fleet Growth (2000–2026)</h4>
      <canvas id="cumulativeGrowth" height="90"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Growth Acceleration: The Numbers</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Year</th><th class="num">Starlink Launched</th><th class="num">Non-Starlink Launched</th><th class="num">Total Launched</th><th class="num">Cumulative Fleet</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>2018</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">200</td><td class="num">200</td><td class="num">1,263</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2019</td><td class="num">17</td><td class="num">134</td><td class="num">151</td><td class="num">1,414</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2020</td><td class="num">298</td><td class="num">200</td><td class="num">498</td><td class="num">1,912</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2021</td><td class="num">625</td><td class="num">438</td><td class="num">1,063</td><td class="num">2,975</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2022</td><td class="num">1,486</td><td class="num">318</td><td class="num">1,804</td><td class="num">4,779</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2023</td><td class="num">1,812</td><td class="num">516</td><td class="num">2,328</td><td class="num">7,107</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2024</td><td class="num">1,874</td><td class="num">603</td><td class="num">2,477</td><td class="num">9,584</td></tr>
      <tr class="highlight-row"><td>2025</td><td class="num">3,160</td><td class="num">1,169</td><td class="num">4,329</td><td class="num">13,913</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2026*</td><td class="num">811</td><td class="num">151</td><td class="num">962</td><td class="num">14,875</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p style="font-size:0.82rem;color:var(--muted);">* 2026 data covers Q1 only (through late March).</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Headline finding:</strong> The active fleet has grown <strong>10.5× since 2019</strong> (1,414 → 14,875). The year 2025 alone added 4,329 currently active satellites — more than the entire fleet size in 2022. Starlink accounts for roughly 73% of this growth. Even excluding Starlink, non-Starlink launches tripled from ~200/year (2018–2020) to ~600–1,169/year (2024–2025), signaling broad industry acceleration — not just one operator.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-box full">
      <h4>Starlink vs. Non-Starlink Annual Launches (2015–2026)</h4>
      <canvas id="starlinkVsRest" height="100"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 5: Orbital Characteristics -->
<div class="section" id="orbital-characteristics">
  <h2>5. Orbital Parameter Deep Dive</h2>

  <h3>5.1 Inclination Distribution</h3>
  <p>Orbital inclination reveals the latitude coverage strategy of the fleet. The distribution is strongly multimodal, with peaks corresponding to specific constellation shells and mission types.</p>

  <div class="chart-row">
